Changelog draft requirements:

- Draft only from `{{ tag_range }}` commits plus authored `## Unreleased`
  content in the fact sheet. Recent merged PRs are context, not release-note
  evidence.
- Use PR descriptions, touched file paths, docs, tests, and representative
  diffs to infer user-visible behavior. Do not copy commit titles as bullets
  unless no richer evidence exists.
- Mention the first post-tag commit `{{ first_post_tag_commit }}` in the Audit
  bullets when it is not `(none)`.
- If there are no post-tag commits and no authored Unreleased entries, the
  draft block should contain only `## {{ target_version }}`.
- Promote authored Unreleased entries under `## {{ target_version }}` and add
  missing release-note-worthy coverage around them.
- Cover user-visible, harness-writer-visible, install, package, publish,
  release, CLI, protocol, stdlib, and behavior changes. Omit routine dependency
  bumps, CI churn, and low-level plumbing that does not change release notes.
- Keep the draft concise: prefer 6-10 top-level bullets, use 12 as the normal
  hard cap, and never exceed 20.
- Use only CHANGELOG markdown between the markers. The heading must be
  `## {{ target_version }}`, not `## Unreleased`.
- Do not use Markdown code fences, angle-bracket placeholders, or literal
  compact tool-protocol tags. Say "compact text-tool aliases" if needed.
- Respect markdown lint wrapping in prose bullets.
